{"command": "cd /testbed && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\np=\"specs/001-transactions/research.md\"\ns=open(p).read()\nnew_items=\"\"\"## R8. invalidate_many any/all semantics (R04)\n\n- **Decision**: `invalidate_many(tags, mode='any')` validates `mode` in\n  `{\"any\", \"all\"}` and validates `tags` with the exact same rules as\n  `put` (bare string rejected, finite iterable of str, dedup via\n  frozenset, generators consumed once). Matching: `any` -> keys in the\n  union of `_tag_keys[t]` over requested tags; `all` -> intersection\n  (keys present in every requested tag's set, computed as\n  `set.intersection(*sets)` over non-empty sets). Empty requested tag\n  set -> returns 0 and removes nothing in BOTH modes (explicitly\n  supersedes the old design note \"empty all-tags matches every key\").\n  Returns the number of distinct removed keys. Removal reuses the same\n  `_remove(key)` choke point as `invalidate`.\n- **Rationale**: Union/intersection over the existing `tag -> set[key]`\n  index gives O(sum of tag set sizes) matching with O(removed) mutation;\n  reusing `_remove` guarantees invariant 3 (no stale memberships).\n  `invalidate(tag)` is implemented as `invalidate_many((tag,), 'any')`\n  semantics (identical result), keeping one code path.\n- **Alternatives considered**:\n  1. Scan all live keys and test tag sets per key - rejected: O(n)\n     per call vs O(matched) with the index.\n  2. `all` mode with empty set matches every key (old design note) -\n     REJECTED per R04: empty removes zero in either mode; the old prose\n     is superseded and SC-006 requires a test proving it.\n\n## R9. resize mechanics (R05)\n\n- **Decision**: `resize(maxsize)` validates capacity with the R4 rule\n  FIRST. On success: if shrinking, evict via repeated\n  `self._cache.popitem()` (public LRU method) routed through\n  `_remove(key)` until `len(self._cache) <= maxsize`; then update the\n  internal capacity attribute (`self._cache._LRUCache__maxsize` - the\n  name-mangled backing field of the `maxsize` property, only write\n  target that avoids rebuilding). Growing: update the capacity\n  attribute only; no eviction. On failure: `ValueError`, zero state\n  change. The `maxsize` property on TaggedCache delegates to\n  `self._cache.maxsize`, so it reports the current capacity after\n  resize.\n- **Rationale**: `popitem()` evicts in true LRU order (the only\n  observable recency order - `__iter__` is insertion order, observed).\n  Updating the backing attribute preserves every surviving entry object\n  and its recency position exactly (R05 + round-2 clarification: no\n  re-copying, no recomputation of any per-entry state). Rebuilding a\n  new LRUCache would re-insert keys in iteration (insertion) order,\n  silently corrupting recency - rejected.\n- **Alternatives considered**:\n  1. Rebuild `self._cache = LRUCache(new_maxsize)` and re-put\n     survivors - rejected: breaks LRU order (iteration is insertion\n     order) and re-copies values.\n  2. Expose a `maxsize.setter` on `Cache`/`LRUCache` - rejected: would\n     modify existing upstream files (Constitution Principle I).\n- **Risk note (Principle V)**: writing the name-mangled attribute is\n  the one coupling point to LRUCache internals; it is confined to\n  `tagged.py`, guarded by a single helper, and covered by a\n  shrink/grow/failed-resize test asserting full state preservation\n  (order verified via subsequent eviction order).\n\n## R10. Generator tags and detachment (R06)\n\n- **Decision**: Tag validation iterates the supplied iterable exactly\n  once (`list(iter(tags))` after the bare-string guard) - a generator is\n  consumed once and never re-iterated; on any validation failure nothing\n  is stored and the cache is unchanged. Detachment: `deepcopy` on input\n  and output (R6 decision) already guarantees returned values stay\n  detached across repeated gets and puts, including nested\n  lists/dicts; the milestone-2 requirement adds no new mechanism, only\n  explicit tests (mutate a returned nested list, re-get, assert\n  isolation; repeat after puts).\n- **Rationale**: One consumption pass is the only reading of \"consumed\n  only once\" and the only one that is safe for one-shot generators;\n  `deepcopy` is the established detachment mechanism (R6).\n- **Alternatives considered**: accept an already-materialized list only -\n  rejected: spec explicitly allows generators.\n\n## Open items\n\nNone remaining. R5 and R9 carry risk notes that are tracked test\nrequirements (explicit tests), not unresolved questions. The Claim-A\nround-2 expiry clarification (R07/R08) is recorded in spec.md\nAssumptions: no expiry attribute exists in the frozen requirement set,\nso resize preserves entry state as-is; revisit if a TTL milestone lands.\n\"\"\"\nanchor=\"## Open items\n\nNone remaining. R5's risk note is a tracked test requirement, not an\nunresolved question.\"\nassert anchor in s\ns=s.replace(anchor,new_items,1)\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nprint(\"research.md lines:\", s.count(chr(10)))\nPYEOF", "exit_code": 1, "stderr": "  File \"<stdin>\", line 85\n    anchor=\"## Open items\n           ^\nSyntaxError: unterminated string literal (detected at line 85)\n", "stdout": ""}